title font5x7 -- the closing capitals, uppercase Latin-1 set closed, GREEN
prompt Autonomous loop -- continue the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; the prior lap named the last uppercase gap; close it.
think The prior lap (font5x7_upper_icelandic) found the block's last uppercase gap by measurement: E diaeresis (U+00CB), I diaeresis (U+00CF), O tilde (U+00D5) stood undrawn though their lowercase twins ship. Closing a whole named set is Lindy-durable floor code (rota this lap deep-read foundations/README.md -- the why beneath the how), so this is the crux-first next rung.
think Studied the mark shapes already shipped: upper_latin1's A diaeresis and O diaeresis carry two parted dots on the top mark row (.#.#. then blank), A tilde carries a wave (..#.# over .#.#.). Reused those exact marks over the compressed capital E, I, O bodies (the accented-capitals rows-2..6 pattern), so the new corpus stays one hand with the letters beside it.
think Built image/font5x7_upper_close.rye with the family idiom: drawings-are-the-spec, explicit ascending codepoints, gfa1 round-trip byte-identical, per-mark toothed guards. The diaeresis guard reads two dots with a blank center gap and a clear second row; the tilde guard reads a two-row wave cresting right. Added a family-consistency proof -- the E and I diaeresis mark rows are byte-identical, so the mark cannot drift between the two letters it crowns.
obs GREEN on the first metal run, no red; ink band 11..17 of 35 (I diaeresis lightest, E diaeresis heaviest). tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII (numeric codepoint literals, the ascii-first font-table exception). Milestone reached honestly: with these three, every alphabetic codepoint in the whole Latin-1 Supplement block -- lowercase and uppercase alike -- now stands drawn in our own open hand. The uppercase Latin-1 letter set is closed.
